title: The Dispensary
start: start

== start ==
bg office_night
music room_tone
The dispensary is the back half of a shop that shut at six. The front half is
dark and full of shelves, and the shutters are down, and the only light is the
one over the counter because the shop's lights are on a timer you do not control.
The phone on the counter is for the answerphone, which is for people who ring
when you are closed. Tonight it rings anyway, which it is not supposed to do,
because you are here and the machine knows when somebody is here.
-> pick_up

== pick_up ==
You have been a pharmacist for twenty-six years and you have never once
answered this phone after ten.
It is ten past one.
* Answer it. -> answer
* Let it ring. -> let_ring

== let_ring ==
It rings eleven times and stops.
You stand with your hands on the bench and you count them, which is a thing you
have done since you were a boy and could not sleep: counting is the only
treatment for waiting that never needs a prescription.
At 01:14 it rings again.
* Answer it. -> answer
* Let it ring a second time. -> let_ring_twice

== let_ring_twice ==
Eleven times again. Then nothing until 01:31, when it rings and does not stop
ringing, and by the fortieth ring you have decided that a man who counts to
forty and does nothing is a man who has stopped being any use to anybody.
-> answer

== answer ==
you: Dispensary.
voice: I'm sorry. I know what time it is.
you: The shop's shut.
voice: I know. I saw the light.
You look at the window in the door that separates the front of the shop from
the back, and there is nobody at it, and the shutters are down.
you: Where are you ringing from?
voice: The car park.
-> the_car_park

== the_car_park ==
You go to the door. You do not open it. Through the glass and the shutters
there is a car with its lights off, and somebody in the driver's seat with a
phone against their ear, and they raise one hand when they see you.
voice: My daughter's prescription. It was meant to be collected on Tuesday.
We couldn't. My wife's been in the hospital since Tuesday.
you: I can't dispense through a door.
voice: I know. I don't want you to. I want to know if it's still there.
-> the_question

== the_question ==
The prescription is in the drawer behind you, in the plastic tray with the
others nobody came for. You know it is there. You put it there yourself on
Tuesday and you wrote the date on the bag in your own hand.
* "It's here." -> its_here
* Go and look, and take your time. -> go_and_look
* "I can't tell you that over the phone." -> cant_tell

== cant_tell ==
you: I can't tell you that over the phone.
voice: It's a box of tablets. It's not a bank.
you: It's a record, and I'm not allowed to read a record out to a voice.
There is a pause long enough that you think the call has dropped.
voice: Then I'll come in the morning.
you: We open at nine.
voice: I know what time you open.
-> the_window

== its_here ==
you: It's here.
voice: Thank you.
That is all. Not a question, not an arrangement, just the two words, and then
the line goes quiet in the way that means the other person has not hung up but
has stopped knowing what to say.
~ told_them true
-> the_window

== go_and_look ==
You go and look, which takes eleven seconds, and you stand at the drawer for
another twenty, because you are buying time for a decision you have not made.
The bag has her name on it and Tuesday's date on it and your handwriting on it,
and it is going to be thrown away at the end of the month if nobody comes.
You come back to the phone.
you: It's here.
~ told_them true
-> the_window

== the_window ==
voice: I'll wait. If that's all right. I'll wait in the car.
you: All night?
voice: Until nine.
You put the phone down. The shop is quiet, and the front is dark, and there is
a car in the car park with a man in it who has decided to spend the night
somewhere he can see a window.
* Put the light out and go home. -> light_out
* Leave the light on. -> leave_it_on
* Make two cups of tea and take one out. -> two_cups

== light_out ==
You put the light out, because the light is not yours and the timer will take
it anyway at half past two, and because a man who sits in a car park all night
looking at a window is a man who has decided something already.
In the morning you find him asleep with the seat back, and you open up at nine,
and the bag is on the counter before he is properly awake.
You do not mention the phone. He does not mention it either.
-> END

== leave_it_on ==
You leave the light on. It costs nothing, it is not yours, and the timer will
take it at half past two whether you leave it or not, which you know and he
does not.
At 02:31 the window over the counter goes dark.
You are still there. You are not sure when you stopped being at work and
started being somewhere else, but the light goes and you are still there, and
so is the car.
-> the_morning

== two_cups ==
You make two cups of tea, because you have been making tea at night for
twenty-six years and your hands do it without being asked.
You take one out to the car park, and he gets out of the car, and neither of you
says anything about the shutters or the light or the prescription. You stand in
the dark and drink tea with a stranger and it is the most ordinary thing that
has happened all night.
~ went_out true
-> the_morning

== the_morning ==
bg office_night
music fan_hum
At twenty past eight he knocks on the back door, which is the door for
deliveries and which nobody knocks on.
He has the same sentence ready that he had on the phone, and you have the bag
ready, and neither of you uses either.
He says his wife is doing better. You say that is good. He asks what he owes and
you say nothing, because it was paid on Tuesday and the shop has already taken
the money, and the only thing that was missing was the handover.
* "Did you tell her it was still here?" -> told_her [if told_them]
* Say nothing more. -> the_close

== told_her ==
He thinks about it properly, which you did not expect at half past eight in the
morning.
He says he told her you said it was still there. He says it was the first thing
he had been able to tell her since Tuesday that was not a date or a form.
You do not say that you broke a rule to say it. He does not ask.
-> the_close

== the_close ==
He goes. You open up at nine. The light over the counter goes off, as it does,
and the machine takes the next call, as it does.
You have worked behind this counter for twenty-six years and you have handed
over thousands of bags, and you have never once thought about what a handover
is for.
* Make the tea and sit down with it. -> END [if went_out]
* Put the kettle on anyway. -> END
